    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d688">

                <pb facs="0864" n="850" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg850"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/3–245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Colombia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wiley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-03T22:00:00-04:00">March 3, 1945—10
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>257. The suggested changes in arrangements for staff conversations with Colombia
                    and Perú contained in your 315, March 2, are unfortunately <pb facs="0865"
                        n="851" xml:id="pg851"/> not practicable. General Brett<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d688-f1"/> is anxious to open all staff conversations himself,
                    leaving a group of officers to carry out the technical conversations after the
                    first 2 or 3 days. This precludes simultaneous conversations in Perú and
                    Colombia. Moreover, the exploratory nature of the conversations makes
                    telegraphic consultation between groups in different countries unnecessary,
                    while the system of separate teams has been devised partly in order to avoid the
                    feeling in any country that U.S. officers were taking military information from
                    one country to a neighboring republic. Finally, several countries have now
                    accepted the dates suggested by this Government in accordance with a
                    carefully-worked out schedule, so that any significant change would involve
                    rearrangement of the schedule and dates already agreed upon.</p>
                <p>It is not believed that there are any grounds for Colombian sensibilities being
                    ruffled by present arrangements once the Colombians know that conversations are
                    definitely scheduled.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d688-f1" n="12" xml:id="d688-f1-content">
                <p>Lt. Gen. George H. Brett, Commanding General, Caribbean Defense Command.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
